  "divergence_findings": [
    {
      "band_id": "band_5_maibang_harangajao_balachera_hill",
      "observation": "light_goods routes this leg in 145600m; rigid_truck and emergency both route it in 168773m (168773m for emergency, effectively identical to rigid_truck). This is the only band of the six where the three profiles do not follow the same physical path.",
      "root_cause_check": "road_access, hgv, max_height, and max_weight path-detail coverage on this leg is IDENTICAL across all three profiles (road_access=yes throughout, hgv=missing throughout, max_height/max_weight=None/unset throughout for every profile -- see by_profile entries above). There is no access/height/weight/hgv restriction tag on the shorter path that would explain rigid_truck or emergency being unable to use it.",
      "conclusion": "This divergence is a ROUTING-PREFERENCE effect of the custom models' distance_influence values (light_goods=90, strongly prefers the shortest path by raw distance; rigid_truck=1 and emergency=30, both weight priority/speed far more than raw distance), not a hard-constraint exclusion. It is disclosed here because it is a real, measured divergence between the intended single NH-27 alignment and each profile's actual computed path, and because it shows GraphHopper choosing a materially different real road for otherwise-identical vehicle legality inputs. It is not evidence of an access/legality restriction; it is evidence that this hill band offers more than one physically routable option in this OSM extract, and profile distance-preference (not vehicle legality) picks between them here."
    }
  ],
